1 NOTE {geni:about_me} [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jacob_ben_Asher '''JACOB ben ASHER'''], also known as Ba'al ha-Turim as well as Rabbi Yaakov ben Raash (Rabbeinu Asher), was probably born in the Holy Roman Empire at Cologne about 12
2 CONC 69 and probably died at Toledo, then in the Kingdom of Castile, about 1343.
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://www.jewishencyclopedia.com/articles/8391-jacob-ben-asher '''JACOB ben ASHER'''] was an influential Medieval rabbinic authority. He is often referred to as the Baal ha-Turim ("Master of the Rows"), after his main work in ha
2 CONC lakha (Jewish law), the Arba'ah Turim ("Four Rows"). The work was divided into four sections, each called a "tur," alluding to the rows of jewels on the High Priest's breastplate. He was the third son of the Rabbi Asher ben Jehiel
2 CONC  (known as the "Rosh"), a Rabbi of the Holy Roman Empire who moved to Castile. Besides his father, who was his principal teacher, Jacob quotes very often in the Turim his elder brother Jehiel; once his brother Judah (see Tur Orach
2 CONC  Chaim, § 417), and once his uncle Rabbi Chaim (ib. § 49). According to many,[by whom?] Jacob moved to Castile with his father and was not born there.
2 CONT
2 CONT Some say Jacob succeeded his father as the rabbi of the Jewish community of Toledo (Zacuto), while others say his brother Judah ben Asher did. His brothers were also rabbis of different communities in Iberia. He lived in abject po
2 CONC verty most of his life, and according to the Sephardic Community of Chios, is said to have fallen ill and died with his ten companions on the island of Chios, in Greece, whilst travelling.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://he.chabad.org/library/article_cdo/aid/3327269
