1 NOTE {geni:about_me} A group of early modern manuscripts from the Jewish communities of Chełm preserves the genealogical chain linking Rabbi Eliyahu Baal Shem to the later Kelma and Fraenkel families. The Pinkas Kehillat Chełm (NLI Heb
2 CONC . Ms. 8° 412) identifies Rabbi Eliyahu Baal Shem explicitly as “Eliyahu son of Aharon Yehuda of Chełm,” establishing the foundational generation. The Sefer HaYuchasin of Chełm (NLI Heb. Ms. 4° 1782) continues this line with his so
2 CONC n, Rabbi Aharon Yehuda Kelma, while the fragmentary Megillat Chełm (NLI F 12345) records the next generation, Rabbi Eliyahu Aharon Kelma, and even mentions a descendant named Uri Lipman, corresponding to Rabbi Uri Lipman Fraenkel.
2 CONC  A marginal gloss in a manuscript of the Maharshal’s Responsa (NLI Heb. Ms. 8° 623) further confirms that the Eliyahu of Chełm mentioned in the responsum is indeed “Eliyahu Baal Shem.” Taken together, these manuscripts form a cohe
2 CONC rent documentary chain supporting the lineage: Baal Shem → Aharon Yehuda → Eliyahu Aharon Kelma → Uri Lipman Fraenkel.
