1 OCCU Rabbi of Grodno and Pinsk, Vienna, Nickolsburg & Metz, grand rabbin à Metz, Rabbi in Prague, Rov and Av Beis Din of Nikolsburg, presiding judge, Av Beis Din Nikelsburg, Rav in Nemirov, Lubomel, Belz, Horodna, Pinsk, Metz
1 TITL Rabbi
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://web.archive.org/web/20071004201110/http://www.yarzheit.com/rebyona.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT * Wikipedia: [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jonah_Teomim-Frankel Jonah Teomim-Frankel] & [https://he.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D7%99%D7%95%D7%A0%D7%94_%D7%AA%D7%90%D7%95%D7%9E%D7%99%D7%9D-%D7%A4%D7%A8%D7%A0%D7%A7%D7%9C יונה תאומים-פרנקל]
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT Rabbi Johnah Theomim (1595 - 16 April 1669) was the author of the noted work on Talmudic commentary "''Kikayon D'yonah''" (edited in Amsterdam 1690 by his son Josua). Johnah Theomim was born in Prague and acted as rabbi in Grodno 
2 CONC and Pinsk (Lithuania). In 1648, because of the Chmielnick pogrom, he fled to Vienna, Nikolsburg (Austria), and finally Metz (Lorraine). Died on 16 April 1669 (15th of Nissan, 5429).
2 CONT
2 CONT * [http://www.loebtree.com/theomim.html Theomim]
2 CONT * [http://www.hebrewbooks.org/pdfpager.aspx?req=6392&st=&pgnum=86 ענף עץ אבות, שמואל זנוויל כהנא, עמוד 86]
2 CONT * [http://www.hebrewbooks.org/pdfpager.aspx?req=29076&st=&pgnum=9 יכבד אב, עמוד 9]
2 CONT * [http://www.hebrewbooks.org/pdfpager.aspx?req=36762&pgnum=206 כלילת יופי, חלק שני, עמוד 206]
2 CONT
2 CONT "''Die Grabschriften des alten Judenfriedhof in Wien''" - Dr. Bernhard Wachstein, Band I pg. 481- here he appears as the son of Moses Ahron Theomim.
2 CONT
2 CONT Author of Kikayon Deyona: http://www.otzar.org/wotzar/book.aspx?101573
2 CONT
2 CONT haGaon Jona Teomim author of Kikajon De Jona son-in-law of haGaon Meir Wahl 
2 CONT
2 CONT Metz Memorbuch #280 (folio 33a), English Translation: The great scholar, the pious, the illustrious... the man of God, the president of the rabbinical court, Chief Rabbi Jonas Teomim of Prague, who exercised the rabbinate for more
2 CONC  than fifty years in the great communities of Nemirov, Lubamla, Belz, Horodno, Pinsk and elsewhere. He trained many of his students, who are now famous scholars. His dialectic dominated his time. When God shook the Jews of the kin
2 CONC gdom of Poland by uninterrupted misfortunes, and they were subjected to exile and vicissitudes, the dove flew to Vienna, where the most illustrious Rabbi Cheftel Levi reigned... son of Rabbi Isaie, as well as the illustrious and s
2 CONC ubtle Rabbi Hechel... From there he came to Metz where he found a refuge... he was invited to Posen and did not want to go there, because here he could teach in peace. He left a rich legacy, his book entitled "The Terebinth of Jon
2 CONC ah" where subtleties abound..." Died and buried on the first day of Passover 5429 [16 April 1669]
2 CONT
2 CONT Translated with www.DeepL.com/Translator (free version)
2 CONT
