1 NAME Simon ben Elieser /Günzburg/
2 GIVN Simon ben Elieser
2 SURN Günzburg
2 _MARNM Ulmo-Günzburg
2 NICK Ba'al Tzemach David
1 NAME שמעון //
2 GIVN שמעון
2 SURN אולמא - גינזבורג
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 1506
2 ADDR
3 CITY Prague
3 STAE Kingdom of Bohemia
3 CTRY Austria
1 DEAT
2 DATE 9 JAN 1585
2 PLAC Günzburg
2 ADDR
3 CITY Günzburg an der Donau
3 STAE Österreichische Markgrafschaft Burgau
3 CTRY Österreichisches Gebiet
1 OCCU parnas à Günzburg et Burgau, Rich Merchant, Rabbi
1 TITL Rabbi
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Schimon Ben Elieser Ulmo wurde 1506 in Günzburg geboren und war ein rabbinischer Gelehrter, Haupt der angesehenen Jeschiwa in Burgau und zugleich auch kaiserlicher Hoffaktor in der österreichischen Marktgrafschaft.
2 CONC
2 CONT
2 CONT Sein Vater Elieser (1477-1544) stammte als Urenkel des Falk ben Lemlin (1390-1465) ab, dessen Ahnen wiederum auf die Familie der Kalonymos zurückgeht und im Jahre 1439 die Reichsstadt Augsburg zusammen mit seinem Bruder Baruch (13
2 CONC 84-1459) samt familiären Anhang verlassen hatte und nach Ulm zog.
2 CONT
2 CONT An seinem Geburtsort Günzburg war Schimon nach der Herkunft seines Vaters noch als Ulma bekannt, in Burgau jedoch wegen seines eigenen Geburtsorts nun mit dem Beinamen Ginzburg oder Günzburg. Sein eigener Name Schimon wurde geläuf
2 CONC ig auch als Simon aufgefasst und zudem verfügte er über den „Spitznamen“ Seligmann. Wegen seines hohen Bekanntheitsgrades, Reichtums und Einfluss gilt Schimon allgemein als Stammvater der sehr weitverzweigten Familie der Ulmo-Günz
2 CONC burg (gelegentlich nach dem alten Stadtnamen auch Ulma, später auch Ulman, Ullman, Ulmann, usw. sowie Ginzburg, etc).
2 CONT
2 CONT (Additional Info from Alfred LUGERT Feb 21 2024)
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://www.jewishencyclopedia.com/articles/6955-gunzburg-ulma-simon-ben-eliezer     '''GÜNZBURG-ULMA, SIMON BEN ELIEZER]: German scholar; communal worker; born at Günzburg, Bavaria, 1506; died at Burgau Jan. 9, 1585. He was the f
2 CONC irst who adopted and transmitted to his descendants the name "Günzburg" as a family name. He was a rich merchant, and traveled around in Germany and Poland in the interests of his business. He was also a great Talmudist, and had s
2 CONC ome knowledge of secular sciences. 
2 CONT
2 CONT It is probably owing to these facts that Simon Günzburg is variously described by different historians. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Albertrandy, quoted by Sternberg ("Gesch. der Juden in Polen," p. 148), says: "Simon, also called Selig Günzburg, was known as a celebrated architect and geometer. He wrote many works, and was the head of the rabbinate and yeshiba
2 CONC h." It seems that Albertrandy confused Simon Günzburg with the physician Selig Günzburg of Slutsk. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Czacki cites him as the court physician of King Sigismund August and chief of the community of Posen (Grätz, "Gesch." ix. 448). But Simon Günzburg never settled at Posen. His residence was first at Günzburg, where, he built a syna
2 CONC gogue and established a cemetery; and then he settled at Burgau, a neighboring town. There also he worked for the welfare of the community, for which reason his name is commemorated in a special prayer.
2 CONT
2 CONT * Bibliography: Sternberg, Geschichte der Juden in Polen, p. 148;
2 CONT * Grätz, Gesch. 3d ed., ix. 448;
2 CONT * Maggid, Toledot Mishpe?ot Gin?burg, pp. 4 et seq.;
2 CONT * David Kaufmann, ib. p. 175.H. R. M. Sel.   -  [http://www.jewishencyclopedia.com/view.jsp?artid=512&letter=G#ixzz1IEvMCeYY ''Source'']
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pfersee  '''The Ulmo (Ginsburg) family]
2 CONT
2 CONT From 1560 until 1875 in Pfersee there was a famous Jewish community with a number of renowned chairmen, rabbis and scholars, mainly from the Ulmo (Ginsburg) family, who until the beginning of the 19th century was in possession of 
2 CONC the so-called “Pfersee handwriting”, the oldest almost complete surviving handwritten edition of the Babylonian Talmud, dated from 14th century, which now is at the Bavarian State Library in Munich. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1627 the Jewish community of Pfersee along with the neighboring Jewish communities of Steppach and Kriegshaber established a common Jewish cemetery, today usually attributed to Kriegshaber, which became part of Augsburg in 1916
2 CONC  as well. [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pfersee  ''Source]
2 CONT
2 CONT >>>>>><nowiki>------------------------------------------</nowiki>
2 CONT
2 CONT n°87 Revue du Cercle de Généalogie Juive p. 15 et 16
2 CONT
2 CONT p. 388 Faustini
2 CONT
2 CONT selon certains, il est né à Gunzburg, Germany?
2 CONT
2 CONT ancêtre des Ulmo-Günzburg, dont le berceau est Pfersee et Burgau
2 CONT
2 CONT nombreuse postérité, 8 fils et 8 filles selon certains auteurs, dont :
2 CONT
2 CONT - Moïse Abraham 
2 CONT
2 CONT - Ella, décédée à Francfort en 1594, épouse d'Akiba Frankfurter, ancêtre des Gugenheim cf n°87 revue CGJ p. 16
2 CONT
2 CONT - Chana 
2 CONT
2 CONT - Abraham 
2 CONT
2 CONT - Salomon
2 CONT
2 CONT - Isaac 
2 CONT
2 CONT - Eliezer
2 CONT
2 CONT - Samuel ...
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Rabbi.
2 CONT He moved to Frankfurt in 1560.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT [http://jhva.wordpress.com/2010/03/11/simon-ulmo-ginzburgs-explanation-of-proverbs-from-the-talmud/ '''Rabbi Simon ben Eliezer Ulmo Ginzburg'''] (1506-1585) who also was a court Jew is a descendant from former Augsburg branch of t
2 CONC he Kalonymos as well as the ancestor of the wide-spread family of Ulmo Ginzburg. He also was famous as architect who built a synagogue and a cemetery in Burgau.  Some of his sons moved to Pfersee what later became the renowned sea
2 CONC t of the rabbinate of Medinat Shwaben. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Leader of the Swabian Jewish Community for over 40 years. Event 1544 Received a patent of protection from Emperor Karl V  [http://wc.rootsweb.ancestry.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=GET&db=lorenfamily&id=I65816&style=TEXT  ''Source'']
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
