2 CONT
2 CONT *[http://www.jewishencyclopedia.com/articles/10581-meir-of-rothenburg-meir-b-baruch  Meir of Rothenburg (Meir B. Baruch)]  ''Read Complete Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT . . .  Meir's first official teach was '''Isaac ben Moses of Vienna''', under whom, as well as under '''Samuel ben Menahem''', he studied at Wurzburg.  Meir studied at the French yeshivoth also, his teachers there being '''Jehiel 
2 CONC ben Joseph of Paris, Samuel ben Solomon of Falaise''', and '''Samuel of Evreux'''.  On his return to Germany he quickly gathered around him a band of devoted pupils.
2 CONT
2 CONT The designations "Chief" and "Father of Rabbis," indicate is great scholarship.  As far as is known, he officiated as rabbin in the following communities:
2 CONT
2 CONT * Kostnitz,
2 CONT * Augsburg,
2 CONT * Würzburg,
2 CONT * Rothenburg,
2 CONT * Worms,
2 CONT * Nuremberg,
2 CONT * Mayence.
2 CONT
2 CONT He is generally called Meir of Rothenberg.  The Weil family of southern n Germany claimed Meir as its ancestor, and there were also many Jews at Prague who designated themselves as his descendants. (Nneubauer, "La Famille de Meir 
2 CONC de Rothenburg". [http://www.jewishencyclopedia.com/articles/10581-meir-of-rothenburg-meir-b-baruch . . . ''More]
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Meir_of_Rothenburg  '''Meir of Rothenburg] (c. 1215 – 2 May 1293)  '''Rabbi Meïr ben Solomon of Perpignan,''' referred to Rab Meir of Rothenberg, as the''' "greatest Jewish leader of Zarfat"''' alive 
2 CONC at the time, Zarfat is medeival Hebrew for France which was a reference to Charlemagne's rule of Germany.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Biography'''
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1286, King Rudolf I instituted a new persecution of the Jews, declaring them servi camerae ("serfs of the treasury"), which had the effect of negating their political freedoms. Along with many others, Meir left Germany with fam
2 CONC ily and followers, but was captured in Lombardy and imprisoned in a fortress near Ensisheim in Alsace.
2 CONT
2 CONT Tradition has it that a large ransom of 23,000 marks silver was raised for him (by the '''Rosh'''), but Rabbi Meir refused it, for fear of encouraging the imprisonment of other rabbis. He died in prison after seven years. Fourteen
2 CONC  years after his death a ransom was paid for his body by '''Alexander ben Salomon Wimpfen''', who was subsequently laid to rest beside the Maharam.[
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Works'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Rabbi Meir wrote many notes, commentaries, expositions, and poems—as well as 1,500 responsa. His disciple the Rosh ('''Rabbi Asher ben Jehiel''') codified much of his teaching.
2 CONT * 	•	His responsa are of great importance to advanced students of the Talmud, as well as to students of Jewish life and customs of those days, especially for the picture which they give of the condition of the German Jews, and of 
2 CONC their sufferings from the caprice of princes and from heavy taxation. These responsa also contain rulings of other older and contemporary Ashkenazi poskim; see History of Responsa: Thirteenth century.
2 CONT * 	•	Rabbi Meir is well known as a Tosafist and in particular, authored the Tosafot commentary of the Talmudic tractate Yoma; he is quoted in the Tosafot on various other tractates. He also authored commentaries on the Tohorot and
2 CONC  Zeraim orders of the Mishnah.
2 CONT * 	•	Rabbi Meir wrote a number of liturgical poems ("piyyutim").
2 CONT * 	•	His writings on specific areas of Halakha (Jewish Law) include:
2 CONT * 	•	Piske Eruvin on the laws of the Eruv;
2 CONT * 	•	Halachoth Pesukoth a collection of decisions on controversial points of Jewish law;
2 CONT * 	•	Hilchoth Berachot on the blessings;
2 CONT * 	•	Hilchoth Avelut on the laws of mourning;
2 CONT * 	•	Hilchoth Shechitah on the ritual slaughtering of animals for Kosher meat.
2 CONT
2 CONT >>>>>><nowiki>-----------------------------</nowiki>
2 CONT
2 CONT HAGGAHOT MAIMUNIYYOT, a comprehensive halakhic work which is one of the most important sources for the halakhic rulings of the scholars of Germany and France. The author, Meir ha-Kohen of Rothenburg (end of the 13th century), was 
2 CONC the distinguished pupil of *Meir b. Baruch of Rothenburg. He compiled it as a supplement and notes (see *Haggahot) to the Mishneh Torah of Maimonides, and its first part was published in the Constantinople edition of the Mishneh T
2 CONC orah .
2 CONT
2 CONT The chief aim of the author was to attach the rulings of the scholars of Germany and France to the work of Maimonides, whose decisions and conclusions are in the main based upon the traditions and rulings of the scholars of Spain.
2 CONC  This aim was the result of the great preoccupation with Maimonides' work in the school of Meir of Rothenburg (who also compiled works connected with Maimonides – see Urbach, 434ff.), as well as the need felt to adapt the work of 
2 CONC Maimonides, which was spreading more and more as a comprehensive halakhic work, for use also in Germany and France.
2 CONT
2 CONT There are differences between the editions of 1509 and 1524, some of which are material. The wording of the glosses in the Venice edition (from which the later editions were printed) is more original and the author generally speak
2 CONC s in the first person, while the wording of the 1509 edition shows signs of being a later version, and has obviously passed through adaptation and abbreviation at the hands of a later editor.
2 CONT In many places in the 1509 edition the passages end with the words: "thus far the language of R.M.K." (= R. Meir ha-Kohen); the editor even comments on the words of Meir ha-Kohen (see Hilkhot Zekhiyyah u-Mattanah 11:19; "however m
2 CONC ay the All-Merciful pardon Meir ha-Kohen…").
2 CONT
2 CONT Certain passages appear in the Constantinople version which are absent from the Venice version, and vice versa. The Constantinople edition contains additions that may have been added by the editor, most of them taken from the Sefe
2 CONC r Mitzvot Gadol (Semag) of '''Moses of Coucy,''' the Sefer Mitzvot Katan (Semak) of '''Isaac of Corbeil''', the Sefer ha-Terumah of '''Baruch b. Samuel of Mainz''', the Ha-Roke'aḥ of '''Eleazar of Worms''', the Seder Olam of Simḥa
2 CONC h of Speyer, etc.
2 CONT
2 CONT The section of responsa contains a valuable collection of the responsa of the author's teacher Meir of Rothenburg, which in some cases gives a reading of greater value than other sources, while others are unknown from any other so
2 CONC urce. Also cited in it are responsa by '''Jacob Tam, Isaac b. Samuel ha-Zaken, Samson of Sens''' (copied from the Nimmukim of his pupil '''Jacob of Courson''', who collected them into a book – see Resp. to Ma'akhalot Asurot, no. 1
2 CONC 3), and his brother '''Isaac b. Abraham, Simḥah b. Samuel of Speyer, Baruch b. Samuel of Mainz,''' etc.
2 CONT
2 CONT No biographical details of Meir ha-Kohen are known other than that he was the pupil of '''Meir of Rothenburg; Mordecai b. Hillel ha-Kohen''' was his colleague and, according to some, his brother-in-law and colleague (Ishut 9, no. 
2 CONC 1); and he lived in Rothenburg (Responsa to Shofetim, no. 16, where the reading should be "here Rothenburg" and not "in it, in Rothenburg"; see Sefer ha-Parnas (1891), no. 269). [http://www.jewishencyclopedia.com/articles/10581-me
2 CONC ir-of-rothenburg-meir-b-baruch      ''Source: Encyclopaedia Judaica.]
2 CONT
