1 OCCU Rabbi, mayor of Horowice, , Houseowner, נלקח מ"מגילות יוחסין עתיקות" בבלוג של בנימין: תולדות ושורשים בעם ישראל, founder of the Horowitz family name, שר העיר הורוביץ, From the city of Ho
1 TITL Rabbi
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} [http://he.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D7%9E%D7%A9%D7%A4%D7%97%D7%AA_%D7%94%D7%95%D7%A8%D7%95%D7%91%D7%99%D7%A5 '''משפחת הורוביץ''']
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://sites.google.com/site/levitedna/paper-genealogy/horowitz-family-trees '''Levite DNA''']
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Horowitz '''The Horowitz Levites Family''']
2 CONT
2 CONT R. Yishayahu ben Moshe Asher Ha-Levi Horowitz  is the founder of Horowitz family name. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He lived in Horovice and later in Prague at the beginning of the 16th century (he died in 1514).
2 CONT
2 CONT R. Yishayahu was a prominent, rich and respected member of Prague Jewish community. He had 7 sons, of whom Aharon Meshulam and Shabtai Sheftel were the best known.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to family tradition (first recorded in the late 19th century), the name Horowitz is associated with Horovice, a small town near Prague in the Czech Republic, where Jews who fled from Spain were allowed to settle. The fou
2 CONC nders of the dynasty were Levites but later non-Levites also entered the family through marriage.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family tradition (first recorded in the late 19th century) has it, that according to scripts from previous centuries, the Halevi family originated in the region of Cataluña, Spain. From among the many family members, most famous i
2 CONC s Rabbi Zarchiya Halevi from Gerona, known as "Baal Hamaor", and we have knowledge of 11 generations of this family in Spain.
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://www.shlomo.horwitzfam.org/in_the_beginning.htm The Beginning of the Horowitz Families]
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://www.horowitzassociation.org/ THE HOROWITZ FAMILIES ASSOCIATION]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''However, other sources doubt the claim of the Spanish pre-Bohemia family lineage of this Halevi Family.''' see [http://www.avotaynuonline.com/2016/03/does-the-horowitz-family-from-bohemia-really-descend-from-the-benvenisti-hale
2 CONC vy-family-from-spain/  Does the Horowitz Family from Bohemia Really Descend from the Benvenisti Halevy Family from Spain?]
2 CONT
2 CONT >>>>----------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT >>=The R1a1a Ashkenazi Levite Y-DNA subclade
2 CONT
2 CONT *[https://sites.google.com/site/levitedna/home '''DNA of the Levitical Horowitz Rabbinical family]
2 CONT
2 CONT This website is devoted to the R1a1a Ashkenazi Levite Y-DNA subclade.
2 CONT Known descendants of the Horowitz Levitical rabbinical family of Prague are R1a1a Ashkenazi Levites.
2 CONT
2 CONT Perhaps half of the R1a1a Ashkenazi Levites who have tested to at least 37 Short Tandem Repeat ("STR") markers appear to share a common ancestor who lived in or about the 15th century.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is believed that this common ancestor – who appears to be the direct male ancestor of more than 1% of Ashkenazim living today – may be the founder of the Horowitz rabbinical family, '''Rabbi Isaiah ben Moshe Asher Halevi Horowi
2 CONC tz''' (c1440 – 1515).
2 CONT
2 CONT Most of the Horowitz men who have tested STR markers to date appear to be descended from the line of Isaiah’s great grandson Pinchas Halevi Horowitz (1535-1618), but we have test results for groups of men who apparently descend fr
2 CONC om Isaiah through other lines (presumably including the line of Isaiah’s grandson the Shelah, Rabbi Isaiah Halevi Horowitz (1568-1627).
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Birth year disputed - 1450, 1465, 1470
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://www.shlomo.horwitzfam.org/where_we_came_from.htm Shlomo Horowitz, Where we came from]
2 CONT
2 CONT ____________
2 CONT
2 CONT Note by direct descendant, Robert Hanscom -- 11 June 2019:
2 CONT
2 CONT His birth year had been previously estimated as "circa 1465".  However, given that his father was born c1435 and his son in c1480, a more reasonable estimate is "circa 1460".  This profile has now been changed to reflect that.
