1 NAME Rabbi Isaac Linnich, A.B.D. Frankfurt
1 NAME אייזיק /ליניך/ אב"ד פפד"מ
2 GIVN אייזיק
2 SURN ליניך
2 NSFX אב"ד פפד"מ
1 SEX M
1 BURI
2 DATE 7 MAR 1556
2 PLAC Battonnstraße Jewish Cemetery
2 ADDR
3 CITY Frankfurt am Main
3 STAE Freie Stadt Frankfurt
3 CTRY Germany
1 DEAT
2 DATE 7 MAR 1556
2 ADDR
3 CITY Frankfurt am Main
3 STAE Freie Stadt Frankfurt
3 CTRY Germany
1 OCCU Rabbi
1 TITL Rabbi
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://www.steinheim-institut.de/cgi-bin/epidat?id=ffb-874
2 CONT
2 CONT I want to be sure to share with you all of the pieces of information we have about Isaac Linnich. He is surrounded by mysteries, but he appears at least six times in Andernacht, and at it looks like Ettlinger found out everything 
2 CONC he could about him.  Still, one wishes there were more.
2 CONT
2 CONT The first mystery, which we have already discussed, is why he is sometimes called Jitzchak von Heyst. Still unsolved, I think.  The second mystery is: Is he really a cousin of Isaac Kronberg (=um 1509) or a more distant relative? 
2 CONC  And is Isaac Kronberg really the brother of Abraham Kronberg (=um1535), who, presumably but not definitely, would then also be a cousin of Isaac Linnich. And one source implies that Abraham Kronberg was a Cohen, and another says 
2 CONC that he is brother of Simon von Geismar (=um1517), who is a Cohen, so at least those two fit nicely as brothers. [Abraham Kronberg's estimated death year of um 1535 is based on a mistaken reference which was really to another Abra
2 CONC ham. Ettlinger had originally given him an estimated death year of um 1508 because that was the year he fled from Frankfurt, so we have no idea at all when Abraham died, except that it was after 1508.]  So we should probably just 
2 CONC keep in mind that Isaac Linnich had relatives in and from Kronberg.
2 CONT
2 CONT Isaac Linnich is mentioned in the following Andernacht records: #3552.24, which is the July, 1508, list shows him listed under Addenda in household 24 (because of the negotiated change described at the end of the entry): Isaac Lyn
2 CONC dich, Zirlin, his wife, and Golde, Meir's mother, a self-sufficient widow, in the house of Isaac Kronberg [household 5, see below] upon the suggestion of Isaac Kronberg and under the same conditions as he had. Isaac von Kronberg h
2 CONC as restricted himself to a fifth of the house in order to pay back his taxes and recover himself financially and (be allowed to) come back to Frankfurt. This arrangement was finalized on 3 Nov 1508, but Andernacht #3561 shows the 
2 CONC text of the original request, which was made 19 Oct 1508: Isaac von Kronberg requests of the city comptroller that he be allowed to take into his home his cousin, Isaac [Linnich], who will be responsible for fuel wood and lighting
2 CONC , which will help him (Isaac Kronberg) be able to pay his yearly taxes; he points out that he has lived in Frankfurt for a long time and never given any cause for complaint, but that he has now fallen behind in the payment of his 
2 CONC overdue taxes. He has lived alone for the past six years, with the exception of a young girl [probably daughter]; he is endeavoring by various means to find a way to pay off the taxes.  Then Andernacht #3563/64 also from Oct 1508 
2 CONC shows Isaac Kronberg's response to some questions which were posed to him by the authorities, apparently to confirm that he had no relatives who could come to his aid financially: Isaac von Kronberg, writing from Worms, informs th
2 CONC e authorities that he has had to take his daughter to her sister in Worms because she could not bear the shame and embarrassment [of his financial downfall]. He just arrived yesterday evening in Worms and as soon as possible will 
2 CONC come back to Frankfurt. Requests that his house be cared for and that the old Jewish books in his room, which are partially the property of his cousin in Windecken, should be turned over to Isaac [?=31 VIII 1518 HO 155?] son of Gu
2 CONC mpel; and he asks for other leniencies to help him toward getting out of debt. He has property and land he is planning to sell, including vineyards and fields in the Koenigstein area. He points out that he has lived in Frankfurt f
2 CONC or 20 years and he goes on to state several complaints about the way he has been treated during the time of his financial problems. He then names individuals who are witnesses to this, including Jonas, Boppelman [=um 1549] the son
2 CONC -in-law of Itzig, Salomon Sack, and Gumpchen. and goes on to complain again about happenings which have affected him and his ability to pay.  The city grants certain parts of his request on 7 November, but it appears that he does 
2 CONC not return to Frankfurt.
2 CONT
2 CONT Returning to the 1508 house list, household 5 as it appeared in July showed Isaac von Kronberg and a wife, if he should he re-marry, and his minor children, in the house unter dem Tanzhaus with the little room located behind the d
2 CONC yer's (Faerber's) house.  Written in the margin is: He has fled. See Addenda: Isaac Lundig. [Shown above].
2 CONT
2 CONT Andernacht #3678.13 is the June 1510 list, and it is one of the lists which only name the head of household, with no further details. His listing reads: #13: Isaac Lynss.
2 CONT
2 CONT So the next drama comes in the July, 1511, list, #3733.1 where we see Isaac as the very first household - and he is sharing it with another family. The entry reads: Zur weissen Rose: Isaac von Iselstein from Holland, Etel, his wif
2 CONC e, Isaac von Lynisz, Zirlin, his wife, Golde, mother of Meir von Worms, for themselfes and their minor children and their servants.
2 CONT
2 CONT Andernacht #3768.1, from June of 1512,and #3909.1 from August 1514 are exact duplicates of the 1511 entry, which is too bad, because there could have been a change or two which were not noted.
2 CONT
2 CONT Andernacht #4087, which is the last one in which Isaac Linnich appears with certainty, is a case of a debt owed to him, where the debtor is requesting relief (which he obtains from Kaiser Maximilian himself). This is dated Decembe
2 CONC r 1516 and is noteworthy because of yet another spelling variation of Isaac's name: Linck.
2 CONT
2 CONT So that's all we know about Isaac Linnich at the moment, but it is very intriguing, don't you think?
2 CONT
