1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Was the "בית שמואל" - one of the Nosei Kailim of the Shulchan Aruch (Even HaEzer).
2 CONT
2 CONT Connected to R' Yitzchak Eizak Chaver via the information in the book:
2 CONT
2 CONT page 536.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Author of "Beis Shmuel" on Shulchan Aruch Even Haezer
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Also known as "Shmuel haRamati", he was chief rabbi of the cities of Szydlow (Poland) and Ferrara (Italy), and is the author of the book "Beit Shmuel".
2 CONT
2 CONT Jews in Szydlow: In 1656 having heard of Hetman Czarniecki military groups marching towards the town, 200 Jews left Szydłów. During the war, the Community moved privileges to Cracow. The war events had a negative effect on the fin
2 CONC ancial state of Community. Out of the 37 Jewish houses thet existed in 1629, some were burnt down. In the year 1660 there were emty building lots and 17 buildings. There were no general shops no cloth shops. Only butchers were mor
2 CONC e active than Catholic ones. The Jewish Community paid taxes on the synagogue and the cemetery. The Jews rented the traiding area from town. anoter confirmation of the former privileges helped undoubtly the economic stabilisation.
2 CONC  In the times before the division of Poland, Szydłow was the site of one of six administrative units called "ziomkostwo krakowsko-sandomierskie" (i.e. Cracow-Sandomierz Unity). Szydłów Jewish Community was under the financial of R
2 CONC abbi of Cracow and under the financial jurisdiction of Cracow administrative unit called "kahał". One of the local Rabbis, on duty in the year 1710-1713, Juda Lejb, was appointed Rabbi in the Cracow Rabbinate. In that time to dist
2 CONC rict to the district of Szydłów  belonged the follwing Jewish Communities: Tarnów, Rymanó, Chmielnik, Pacanów, Oleśnica, Żabno, Dąbrowa, Stopnica, Połaniec, Bogoria, Staszów, Raków, Wiślica and Kurozwęki.
2 CONT
2 CONT Regarding Ferrara: The Jewish community of Ferrara is the only one in Emilia Romagna with a continuous presence from the Middle Ages to the present day. It played an important role when Ferrara enjoyed its greatest splendor in the
2 CONC  15th and 16th century, with the duke Ercole I d'Este. The situation of the Jews deteriorated in 1598, when the Este dynasty moved to Modena and the city came under papal control. The Jewish settlement, located in three streets fo
2 CONC rming a triangle near the cathedral, became a ghetto in 1627. Apart from a few years under Napoleon and during the 1848 revolution, the ghetto lasted until Italian unification in 1859.
2 CONT ----
