0 @N6000000001898749263@ NOTE From http://www.jewishencyclopedia.com/view.jsp?artid=398&letter=H
1 CONT
1 CONT HAYYIM BEN BEZALEEL:   (print this article)    
1 CONT
1 CONT  By : Kaufmann Kohler   A. Peiginsky   
1 CONT
1 CONT German Talmudist; died at Friedberg on the Shabu'ot festival, 1588. He was the eldest of the four sons of Bezaleel ben ?ayyim, and spent his youth at Posen, the native city of the family. (comp. "Monatsschrift," xiii. 371). He and
1 CONC  Moses Isserles studied with Shalom Shakna, whose methods of teaching ?ayyim largely adopted. He began his literary activity at Worms, where he had gone in 1549; and, apparently, he succeeded his uncle Jacob ben ?ayyim as rabbi in
1 CONC  that city, after Jacob's death in 1563 (comp. his introduction to "Mayim ?ayyim," printed in "Ha-Shiloa?," § 9). He subsequently went as rabbi to Friedberg; in 1578 this district was ravaged by a terrible plague, which caused the
1 CONC  death of one of ?ayyim's servants. In consequence of this occurrence ?ayyim and his family were quarantined in his house for two months. During this time he wrote his ethical work "Sefer ha-?ayyim," consisting of five books.
1 CONT
1 CONT ?ayyim carried on a heated controversy with his former schoolfellow Moses Isserles, also indirectly aiming at Joseph Caro. He did not approve of their attempts to collect the laws found in the Talmud and other authoritative works 
1 CONC in a book suitable for the general public. The reasons for his objections he set forth in the introduction to his "Mayim ?ayyim," which includes a criticism of Moses Isserles' "Torat ha-?a??ot." ?ayyim held that through such codic
1 CONC es the study of the Talmud would be neglected and the standing of the rabbis injured, since every layman could turn to these books for the solution of difficult questions. Moreover, the writer of such codes would gain too much aut
1 CONC hority over other teachers, whereas every rabbi ought to arrive at his decisions independently. Such codes, moreover, could not take into account theminhagim of all countries; and this, again, would lead to constraint in matters o
1 CONC f conscience, since every one would have to observe the minhagim obtaining in the place where the author of the code in question was living.
1 CONT
1 CONT ?ayyim's works include: "Sefer ha-?ayyim," Cracow, 1593; Amsterdam, 1713; Lemberg, 1887; "Mayim ?ayyim," Amsterdam, 1711; Lemberg, without introduction; "Iggeret ha-Tiyyul," Scriptural comments in alphabetical order, Prague, 1605,
1 CONC  and Offenbach, 1717; "'E? ha-?ayyim"; "Be'er Mayim ?ayyim," supercommentary to Rashi's commentary on the Pentateuch.
1 CONT
1 CONT Bibliography: Zedner, Cat. Hebr. Books Brit. Mus. p. 177; 
1 CONT Fürst, Bibl. Jud. i. 157; 
1 CONT Azulai, Shem ha-Gedolim, ii. 39; 
1 CONT Michael, Or ha-?ayyim, p. 388; 
1 CONT Ha-Shiloa?, v. 431-439; 
1 CONT Gal 'Ed, p. 7.K. A. Pe.
1 CONT
1 CONT
