0 @N6000000004339589235@ NOTE R. Yishayahu was a prominent and respected member of Prague Jewish community. He had 7 sons, of whom Aharon Meshulam and Shabtai Sheftel were the best known.
0 @N6000000004339589626@ NOTE From Horowitz Family website:
1 CONT
1 CONT The only knowledge of the 6th son of R. Yishayahu, R. Yosef is that the authorities wished to punish him along with his brothers, R. Aharon Meshullam and R. Zeligman. They appealed to the king to consider their case, and the latte
1 CONC r issued a favorable decision on their petition.
0 @N6000000004339589315@ NOTE From Horowitz Family website:
1 CONT
1 CONT The 3rd son, R. Meir was a wealthy and respected man in Prague, not much less than his elder brother, R Aharon Meshulam. He engaged in various financial operations. When in the year 1540 the authorities imposed a heavy tax on the 
1 CONC Jews - 2,000 guldens, the community appealed to the government, arguing that it could not pay such a sum. Then the authorities obliged every Jew to make a statement about his possessions and to swear an oath on a Torah scroll. Thu
1 CONC s 60 wealthy Jews were identified, and each one of them was taxed 50 guldens, R. Meir being one of them.
0 @N6000000004339589181@ NOTE From Horowitz Family website:
1 CONT
1 CONT The 5th son, R. Zeligman, also called Asher apparently after his great grandfather - in 1534 was a leader of Prague's Jewish community. He was a brave man, he was not afraid of anything but had a very bad temper. When he was enrag
1 CONC ed, he could not control himself, even before people more profound in their knowledge of Torah or of higher social standing. In 1534, when there was controversy between the Horowitz family and other community leaders and its rabbi
1 CONC s, an incident happened in which R. Zeligman quarreled in public with the community's rabbi, R. Altshuller. In the heat of the argument R. Zeligman seemed to completely loose his self-control when in the presence of all he slapped
1 CONC  his opponent. This case was brought before King Ferdinand, and he ordered that R. Asher be punished. Another time, during a trial involving some Christian, R. Zeligman clearly saw that the judges were biased and obviously incline
1 CONC d in favor of his opponent. Then R. Asher angrily asked them: "I am a resident of the city, the same as he, am I not? And I am respected and no less important than this Christian! But it was of no avail: the judges retorted that t
1 CONC he Jews were not citizens of Prague, and "one may not even compare them with the Christians!"
0 @N6000000004339589405@ NOTE From Horowitz Family website:
1 CONT
1 CONT The 7th son, R. Shabtai Sheftel, the youngest of all was the most prominent in Torah learning. He was dayan in the Jewish community of Prague and one of its leaders. He was by right reputed to be the best expert on Torah and its m
1 CONC ost enlightened commentator. Thus, no one was surprised when the outstanding rabbi of that time, R. Akiva Katz, consented to give him his daughter Yocheved in marriage. Yocheved delivered 3 sons and 3 daughters and died three year
1 CONC s after her husband, in 1555.
